Q: Best way to handle similar type of function without writing them again in C++ A: <p>You could move the repeated code into a helper function. If I understand the intent of this question, the &quot;calculations that only use 'var' variable&quot; are what you are concerned about repeating. So, use the standard strategy of moving repeated code into its own function. This helper would handle everything your original function does up to the point where <code>func</code> is invoked. (If there was common functionality after invoking <code>func</code>, that could be put into a second helper function; this is left up to the reader.)</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>double BigCalculation(double var) // Consider using a more descriptive name.
{
    // calculations omitted
    return var /*should be var2, but that calculation was omitted */;
}
</code></pre>
<p>At this point, writing a bunch of overloads does not involve so much repetition. (These could be simplified, but I'll keep the variable names for now to make it easier to compare to the code in the question.)</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>double BigFunction(double (*func)(double), double var)
{
    double var2 = BigCalculation(var);
    double var3 = func(var2);
    return var3;
}

double BigFunction(double (*func)(double, double *), double var, double *para)
{
    double var2 = BigCalculation(var);
    double var3 = func(var2, para);
    return var3;
}
</code></pre>
<p>While this is better, it still requires work for each function signature. A function template would let you combine your overloads into one definition. (For this version, I'll simplify away <code>var2</code>. If you also simplify away <code>var3</code>, this becomes a one-liner.)</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template &lt;class... Args&gt;
double BigFunction(double (*func)(double, Args...), double var, Args... params) {
    double var3 = func(BigCalculation(var), params...);
    return var3;
}
</code></pre>
<p>One advantage of this approach is that while <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/495021">templates must be defined in a header</a>, the big calculation can be kept in a source file. If the calculation needs to be modified (as a bug fix?), only one translation unit would need to be re-compiled.</p>
<hr />
<p>Personally, I would be inclined to change the design so that this <code>BigCalculation</code> is what external code would call. It's less work for you, since the <code>BigFunction</code> template would be dropped, and the change to call sites is marginal. Compare calling your version</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>BigFunction(func, var, para)
</code></pre>
<p>to calling the calculation directly and manually invoking <code>func</code></p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>func(BigCalculation(var), para)
</code></pre>
<p>The syntax is similar, and there is no obvious reason to prefer one over the other.</p>
<p>(The situation would be different if there was common functionality <strong>after</strong> invoking <code>func</code> and before returning a value. In that case, there would be two helper functions to call, which adds value to the <code>BigFunction</code> wrapper.)</p>

Q: How can I read a large text file line by line using Java? A: <h2>Java 8+</h2>
<p>Use Java NIO.2 features, including the <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/Paths.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Paths</code></a> and <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/Files.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Files</code></a> utility classes.</p>
<p>Call <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/Files.html#lines(java.nio.file.Path)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Files.lines</code></a> to get a stream a <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/util/stream/Stream.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Stream</code></a> of the lines read from the file. Defaults to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UTF-8" rel="nofollow noreferrer">UTF-8</a> character encoding, with option to <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/Files.html#lines(java.nio.file.Path,java.nio.charset.Charset)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">specify character-encoding</a>.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>String fileName = &quot;path/to/example.txt&quot; ;
try 
    (
        Stream&lt;String&gt; stream = Files.lines(Paths.get(fileName))
    ) 
{
    stream.forEach(System.out::println);
}
</code></pre>
<p>You have to place the <code>Stream</code> in a <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/essential/exceptions/tryResourceClose.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">try-with-resource</a> block to ensure the <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/util/stream/BaseStream.html#close()" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>#close</code></a> method is called on it, otherwise the underlying file handle is never closed until the garbage collector does it much later.</p>
<h2>Earlier Java</h2>
<p>A common pattern is to use <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/io/BufferedReader.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>BufferedReader</code></a>.</p>
<pre><code>try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(file))) {
    String line;
    while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
       // process the line.
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can read the data faster if you assume there is no character encoding. e.g. ASCII-7 but it won't make much difference.  It is highly likely that what you do with the data will take much longer.</p>
<p>EDIT: A less common pattern to use which avoids the scope of <code>line</code> leaking.</p>
<pre><code>try(BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(file))) {
    for(String line; (line = br.readLine()) != null; ) {
        // process the line.
    }
    // line is not visible here.
}
</code></pre>

Q: Is there a way to set the default camera direction when a page loads, in a-frame? A: <p>When using look-control, you can set the camera position with:</p>
<pre><code>const camera = document.querySelector('a-camera, #camera');
camera.components['look-controls'].pitchObject.rotation.set(0,0,0);
camera.components['look-controls'].yawObject.rotation.set(0,0,0);
</code></pre>
<p>Angles are in rad not in deg</p>
